{
  "intro": "Practice networking skills in your browser: subnetting drills, ports and DNS records, and two command-prompt labs that teach you to find the broken layer and decide what to fix or escalate. Everything is graded for you.",
  "labs": [
    {
      "id": "np-subnet",
      "title": "Subnet it",
      "domain": "D2",
      "kind": "tasks",
      "level": "beginner",
      "minutes": 12,
      "summary": "Work out network, broadcast, usable range and host count, then pick the right prefix for each requirement.",
      "scenario": "<p>You are documenting two subnets for a new office and need to choose sizes for three more.</p>",
      "activities": [
        {
          "type": "fill",
          "title": "Document 172.16.40.130/27",
          "prompt": "Fill in each value.",
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Subnet mask",
              "answer": [
                "255.255.255.224"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Network address",
              "answer": [
                "172.16.40.128"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Broadcast address",
              "answer": [
                "172.16.40.159"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "First usable host",
              "answer": [
                "172.16.40.129"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Last usable host",
              "answer": [
                "172.16.40.158"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Usable hosts",
              "answer": [
                "30"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "fill",
          "title": "Document 10.1.5.200/22",
          "prompt": "Fill in each value.",
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Subnet mask",
              "answer": [
                "255.255.252.0"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Network address",
              "answer": [
                "10.1.4.0"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Broadcast address",
              "answer": [
                "10.1.7.255"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Usable hosts",
              "answer": [
                "1022",
                "1,022"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "fill",
          "title": "Choose the prefix",
          "prompt": "Pick the smallest prefix that fits each requirement.",
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "Link between two routers (2 hosts)",
              "answer": [
                "/30",
                "30",
                "/31",
                "31"
              ],
              "placeholder": "/__"
            },
            {
              "label": "Guest Wi-Fi, up to 100 devices",
              "answer": [
                "/25",
                "25"
              ],
              "placeholder": "/__"
            },
            {
              "label": "Server VLAN, 25 hosts",
              "answer": [
                "/27",
                "27"
              ],
              "placeholder": "/__"
            },
            {
              "label": "Office floor with 400 devices",
              "answer": [
                "/23",
                "23"
              ],
              "placeholder": "/__"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tasks": [
        {
          "text": "Subnet 172.16.40.130/27 correctly",
          "check": {
            "activity": 0
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Subnet 10.1.5.200/22 correctly",
          "check": {
            "activity": 1
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Choose the right prefix for each requirement",
          "check": {
            "activity": 2
          }
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "/27 means a block size of 32 in the last octet: 0, 32, 64, 96, 128, 160. Where does .130 fall?",
        "For /22 the interesting octet is the third one: 255.255.252.0 gives a block size of 4. Which block holds 5?",
        "Usable hosts = 2^host bits - 2. A /23 has 9 host bits: 510."
      ],
      "review": "<p>Find the block size, the block your address falls in, then network = block start and broadcast = next start minus 1. Choose the smallest prefix whose usable host count covers your needs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "np-ports",
      "title": "Ports, records and DHCP",
      "domain": "D3",
      "kind": "tasks",
      "level": "beginner",
      "minutes": 10,
      "summary": "Match ports to services and DNS records to jobs, and put the DHCP conversation in order.",
      "scenario": "<p>A new colleague asks how the pieces of name resolution and addressing fit together. Walk through them.</p>",
